# Architecture

## Server vs client components

Default to Server Components. Add `'use client'` only for browser APIs
(`window`, `document`, measurement), React state or effects, or event handlers.

- Never put `'use client'` on a page (`app/**/page.tsx`) or a layout.
- Push interactivity into leaf islands. Each section ships at most one island;
  the island is its own file (`nav.tsx` · `nav-island.tsx`).
- Never call the data-layer fetch functions inside a client component. Server
  components fetch and pass data down as typed props.
- Route JS is measured in hundreds of bytes per page. A new island earns its
  place; a new dependency earns it twice.

## The design system is central

All brand UI lives under `design-system/v2/` and is consumed through it. The
layers build strictly upward — **tokens → base → grid → primitives →
sections** — and a layer may use only the layers before it in that order
(a primitive uses tokens and the grid; it never imports a section).

- `tokens/` — primitives · semantic · type · motion · `component.css` (every
  designed non-token constant). The only place colors, fonts, radii, spacing,
  z-index, and motion values are defined.
- `base.css` — the document reset the sections were verified under and the
  site root `.v2-root` (carried by `body`). Part of the rendering contract; do
  not restyle it casually.
- `grid/` — the engine (`engine.css`), the exposure vocabulary, the side fields.
- `primitives/` — the lowest building blocks (buttons, grader, text, icons in
  `icons.tsx`). Build everything from these; never hand-roll a styled
  `<button>` or `<h2>` in a page or section.
- `sections/` — full-width page sections and the site chrome (nav, footer,
  site chat). A section owns its `*.css`, its `*-data.ts`, and its island.
- `lib/` — shared client utilities (scroll lock, the Grader client), `media.ts`
  — the media registry, `widgets.css` — the compiled stylesheet of the
  `@keystone-sites/widgets` package (see `60-data-and-integrations.mdc`).

All site CSS lives in these layers, assembled by `v2/index.css` in cascade
order. Edit the file for the layer you work on; never add rules to the index.
No CSS modules, styled-components, emotion, `<style>` tags (the root layout's
commented cold-load guard is the sole exception), and no utility-class
framework for site markup.

## Component organization

- One component per file. Named exports only. TypeScript interfaces for every
  component; never `any`, never `object`.
- A section and its parts share a name prefix (`nav.tsx` · `nav-desktop.tsx`
  · `nav-mobile.tsx` · `nav.css` · `nav-data.ts`).
- A file past ~500 lines is a missing folder: split into one file per visually
  distinct sub-component, with a colocated `types.ts`. Content arrays never
  share a file with their component.
- A sub-component is real when it has a discrete prop interface and output and
  can be reasoned about without the parent's render. Pure layout wrappers stay
  inline.

## Pages compose from the design system

A page file mounts design-system sections and feeds them data — nothing more.
A page never defines its own nav, footer, hero chrome, colors, or CSS.

## Everything is prop-driven

- No hardcoded content in components: every string, image, color role, label,
  URL, and configuration is a prop or a typed `*-data.ts` module the component
  imports. This holds even for a single use today.
- Default prop values only for layout or style options (`variant`, `size`),
  never for content.
- Behaviour hooks between sections and islands are declarative
  `data-action="…"` contracts on the primitive (`ButtonGhost action`), wired
  by one delegated document listener in the owning island. Never wire an
  `onClick` across section boundaries.

## State

- **One source of truth.** A piece of state has one owner: React, or the
  timer/animation code that React reads through a ref. Never both. Focus is
  owned by the DOM; uncontrolled inputs own their value.
- **No module-level mutable state.** A top-level `let` or shared `Map` is a
  hidden global that breaks under Strict Mode and across navigations. Use React
  state, refs, or a context whose value is a ref. Module-level `const`
  registries and small bounded caches of pure network answers are the
  exceptions — they must be safe to lose at any time.
- **Prefer native CSS state.** `:hover`, `:focus-within`, `:focus-visible`,
  `:has()`, `data-*` attributes, and container queries carry state the CSS can
  see. JS owns only what it must: timers, offsets, debounced flags, springs.
- **Derive, don't sync.** Status that follows from other state is computed in
  render, not stored. The sanctioned derive-state-while-rendering form
  (`if (cond) setX(...)` in the body) replaces `setState` in an effect.
- **Discriminated unions over string sniffing.** Every variant carries a
  `type` literal; renderers are one exhaustive `switch`. Classify external
  data once at the boundary into a closed union.

## Data models are typed at the boundary

Every external response is validated before it enters the app. Declare the
type once, validate the parsed body against it with a small hand-written
parser, and use the validated value. A bare `as { … }` cast is forbidden at
our boundary; typed third-party SDK responses are trusted.

## Effects are idempotent

Every effect and listener is safe to set up, tear down, and set up again
(Strict Mode double-mounts, HMR, bfcache).

- Always return a cleanup: every `addEventListener` pairs with a remove; every
  timer with a clear; every observer with `disconnect()`.
- Capture `ref.current` into a local at effect start and use the local in the
  cleanup.
- Never depend on the initial mount being the only mount.

"Works the first time, breaks after hot reload" means a missing cleanup, not a
missing `useRef` flag.

## UI coordinates through events, not timers

`setTimeout` is not a synchronisation primitive. Wait for `animationend`,
`transitionend`, `decode()`/`load`, `requestAnimationFrame`, or a synchronous
`focus({ preventScroll: true })` in the right effect. Timers are for things
that genuinely fire on a timer: auto-advance, debounce.

## Body scroll locking

The single entry point is `design-system/v2/lib/scroll-lock.ts`. Every overlay
calls `lockScroll()` and stores the unlock as its cleanup. Never set
`document.body.style.overflow` from a component; never write a parallel helper.

## No raw console logging

Shipped code carries no `console.log`. If diagnostics become necessary, add one
central logger with channels and kill switches. `console.warn`/`error` for
developer-must-see conditions are acceptable, sparingly.
